/spawn no longer deletes a worker's worktree while it still holds work. It's committed as 93e85b55. Build, the full test suite, clippy with -D warnings and the fmt check all pass.

The bug, reproduced first. In a scratch repo I created a worktree the way spawn does and put an untracked file in it. Then I ran the first command the old cleanup used, git worktree remove --force. It exited 0 and the file was gone. Three things led there:
• Failed handoff commit: a pre-commit hook rejects it, signing is required but no agent is running, or user.email isn't set.
• Output-file write failure: cleanup ran before the handoff was even tried.
• Hourly stale sweep: it judges age by the worktree folder's own timestamp, which doesn't change when files inside it are edited.

The fix. Worktree removal now lives in a new file, src/spawn_worktree.rs. The one function that runs worktree remove --force checks the worktree first, so all five removal paths are covered: four from the normal cleanup and one from the stale sweep. The check:
• Uncommitted changes (git status --porcelain not empty): the worktree is kept.
• Commits that no branch, tag or remote points to: kept. This covers a worker that committed itself, or a handoff whose commit landed but whose branch naming failed.
• Folder isn't a worktree of its own: treated as "could not check" and kept. Otherwise git would look up into the parent repo and report its status instead.
• Any check fails: kept and reported as "could not check", never treated as clean.
• Clean: removed exactly as before.

When a worktree is kept, a note always goes to stderr (it isn't hidden by quiet mode). It gives the path, what's in it, how to inspect it (git -C <path> status) and how to discard it (git worktree remove --force <path>). In plain-output mode it has no symbols. The completion messages no longer say "worktree cleaned up" when the worktree was kept.

Spawn never deletes branches, so the handoff branch is always left in place. Ignored files aren't counted as work; the handoff never committed them either.

Tests. There are 8 new tests, all in temp repos:
• dirty worktree kept, with its file intact
• clean worktree removed
• a commit on no branch is kept, and the same worktree is removed once a branch points to it
• check failure keeps the worktree
• a folder that isn't a worktree isn't judged by the parent repo's status
• the stale sweep keeps the dirty one and removes the clean one
• the kept message: exact text, and no symbols in plain mode

I also broke the uncommitted-changes check on purpose and ran the tests. The dirty-worktree and stale-sweep tests failed by name, and passed again once the check was restored.

Other deleting code checked. I searched src/ for worktree remove, remove_dir_all and branch -D. Apart from the new guarded function, every hit deletes a temporary or half-made folder yoyo created itself, or is in a test, or is just a flag in a pattern list. None of them can lose user work.

Still open: #1057, filed. A clean --bg worker that has been running for over an hour can still have its worktree swept away mid-task. Nothing records which worktrees belong to live workers. No work is lost in that case, because a worker that has written anything is protected by the new check.

